// U FR325:1-98 | Farid Responds | 5
#passage(("Christian Prince prides himself on being someone that keeps on surprising us with new fresh arguments against Islam. Well, I'll admit I have heard some things from Christian Prince that I have never heard elsewhere, but that's not necessarily something to be proud of. The changing of the direction of prayer occurred in the second year after the migration to Medina.", "The first lie is that [Umar ibn al-Khattab] was the reason for the direction of prayer to change. CP quotes a hadith [that] speaks about the station of Ibrahim. Now every [Muslim that] has been to Mecca, and pretty much almost all Muslims, know that the station of Ibrahim is this [cage] area. This has nothing to do with direction of prayer. What does it mean to take the station of Ibrahim as a prayer place? Well, it means to pray right here.", "Jews that accepted Islam: perhaps the most famous of them was [Abdullah ibn Salam], who was one of the rabbis. Also notice how CP argues that the Muslims change their direction because the Jews didn't accept Islam; he's going to contradict himself in a bit. A little while ago CP said that the direction of prayer was changed because the Jews didn't accept Islam; now CP is saying that it changed because they killed all the Jews and the Muslims didn't have money.", "This leads us to the fourth lie: the Muslims didn't kill all the Jews in Medinah. Also, don't forget that the event of the changing of the prayer occurred in the second year after the migration, so all the Jews were still in Medina at the time.", "Omar never said such a thing. Keep in mind that Makkah was under control of the polytheist [Meccans] at the time, and remember, the polytheist [Meccans] are the enemies of the Muslims. Why would Allah try to make money for the [polytheists]? That is just dumb.",), "Farid Responds, “Christian Prince: Omar changed Prayer Direction! [Lies #90-94]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=udACAObS4Jg", ((0, "0:00–0:30"), (90, "1:30–1:57"), (149, "2:29–3:30"), (210, "3:30–3:53"), (258, "4:18–4:37"),), id: 2206)
// U FR013:37-86 | Farid Responds | 5
#passage(("The main thing I want to point out that really caught my eye — and again, this is just really mundane information, but something that I thought was really interesting — it's that Sa'd ibn Ubadah did not say, \"Prepare my horse.\" He said, \"Prepare my donkey.\" He didn't even say, \"Prepare my mule.\" He said, \"Prepare my donkey.\" Why is he going on a donkey? Why isn't he going on a horse? Is it one of those things where perhaps the distance wasn't too far off, and therefore you're not supposed to use a horse? But what seems to be going on is that the head of Banu Sa'idah, one of the main bosses of the Khazraj at the time, Sa'd ibn Ubadah, did not have a horse, which shows you the state of the people of Medina. And of course, when we look into battles, especially the early ones, in the Battle of Badr there was like one or two horses involved on the side of the Muslims, which implies that the people of Medina were not wealthy at all, at least nothing compared to the Meccans. And even the Meccans, in many months they would struggle financially.", "Now, another thing that may support this theory is the famous Hadith of Ka'b ibn Malik, in which he did not go to Tabuk, and him and two of the companions go to the Prophet, peace be upon him, and seek repentance. In the very end, when he hears the good news that verses have been revealed forgiving him and the two other companions for not going to Tabuk, what he did was he took off his thobe and he gave it to the person that gave him the good news. Now, he says in the report, and this report you can find, of course, in both Bukhari and Muslim: \"And I did not have another thobe at the time.\" Now, keep in mind, this is Ka'b ibn Malik, who is one of the biggest poets in Medina, and actually the whole Arabian Peninsula. But that just shows you that the people from Medina had it really rough.", "Another one that also suggested that times were really tough in Medina is a narration of Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, which we find again in Bukhari and Muslim, in which he says that the prophet, peace be upon him, said that if you're going to defecate, then do not face the qibla and do not have it behind you; be towards the side or at a diagonal or something. Abu Ayyub al-Ansari — remember he's from Banu Najjar; keep these things in mind, the names of these clans, they're beneficial, makes you enjoy these reports more — he says: \"And then when we went to Sham we found the marahiid that were built facing the Kaaba.\" The marahiid, of course: any Arab today will tell you that a [mirhad] is like a toilet. The report doesn't mention anything much more other than that, but he says we used to use the toilets while leaning towards a different direction.", "What's really interesting here is that Abu Ayyub al-Ansari is speaking of this in a way that indicates that they didn't have toilets in Medina. We could have guessed that, but what's interesting here is that he's saying that they did have them in [al-Sham]. In [al-Sham], these were built in toilets. And in Sham, at least parts of it, perhaps towards the north, they did seem to have some sort of drainage system. Here you have Bosra in [al-Sham], which is of course under Byzantine control. It's a walled city, it's a really good looking city compared to the run down archaic structures that you find in the Hijaz and good chunk of the Arabian Peninsula. You actually do see the Byzantine infrastructure affecting the people of Bosra.", "So, what do we benefit from this? A lot of things. If you connect these dots together and you look at all these matters holistically: even the poets themselves don't have more than one thobe. You have the leader not having a horse, but only having a donkey. The people not having proper toilets. Despite all of that, they were able to succeed in spreading the message and spreading their influence over the whole region. And not only that, but conquer their enemies, defeat their foes from the Byzantines and the Sassanids, which were the two strongest empires at the time. Subhanallah.",), "Farid Responds, “I just noticed something about these hadiths...”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YXz5dQQbBVY", ((199, "3:19–4:39"), (279, "4:39–5:48"), (348, "5:48–6:49"), (415, "6:55–7:46"), (466, "7:46–8:30"),), id: 2207)

