#passage(("This prophecy has to do with Surat al-Ahzab and al-Ahzab in general. I'm referring to the Battle of the Trench. You have Quraysh, you have Ghatafan, you have Banu Qurayzah from behind, from inside the Medina. You have these three groups. They're coming together, cooperating to destroy the Muslims. Quraysh, of course, they've been an enemy of the Muslims from the very beginning. And now all of a sudden, they have the support of Ghatafan. And for those of you that are familiar with the tribes, pre-Islamic tribes, you know that Ghatafan, they're very dangerous. And then, of course, you have within the Medina itself Banu Qurayzah. They're planning to stab the Muslims in the back.", "So, when this occurs, how were the Muslims feeling? How close were they to annihilation? They were going to be completely slaughtered. And the Quran preserves multiple reactions of the people of Medina towards what was going on. One verse, verse number 10, says, \"Remember when they came at you from above you and from below you, and when eyes shifted in fear and hearts reached the throats, and you assumed about Allah various assumptions. There the believers were tested and shaken with a severe shaking.\" Subhanallah, seeing across the trench thousands of people that have come together with the sole intention of killing you is not a small matter.", "In the Battle of the Trench, there are multiple factors that caused the Muslims to not be annihilated and to survive, and the main one is the wind that Allah subhana wa ta'ala sent, and the wind that lasted for a while, and it knocked down their tents, and the Quraysh and the Ghatafan, they were not able to persevere through, and they just had to retreat. So, it is something from Allah subhana wa ta'ala. It is a miracle that you actually find preserved in Surat al-Ahzab, which explains exactly what happened. And the same surah reminds the Muslims to remember Allah subhana wa ta'ala, to remember the blessings that he provided them when they saw the armies, and he himself sent a wind in order to save them.", "Now, during the Medinan period, this was definitely the lowest point that the Muslims had went through. They had, of course, the victory at Badr, and then they were attacked by Quraysh, and the war occurred at Uhud. And then you had this. You had al-Khandaq, which was a battle in which they were in the defensive, and the most that they could wish for is survival. They were vastly outnumbered. Quraysh and Ghatafan were at 10,000, while the Muslims were only at 3,000.",), "Farid Responds, “Underappreciated Prophecy Made in 627 CE”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9VrIY2l0P14", ((39, "0:39–1:33"), (94, "1:34–2:31"), (155, "2:35–3:24"), (206, "3:26–3:59"),), id: 2208)
