#passage(("If someone is saying, \"No, this is wrong. If you do this, you're attacking the prophet,\" then we have a problem. This issue of the age of Aisha, the exact age of Aisha, what age she exactly was: it's not an issue of creed. Whether Muslims believe she was 18, she was nine, she was 12, she was 20, doesn't matter. We have six articles of faith and five pillars. If you believe in the six articles of faith, you believe in the five pillars, you are a Muslim. Of course, it's the thing which most of Islamophobes use against Islam.", "The reason I chose to respond to this one, even though I know this is not a creedal issue, is because of what he says. He says he's a Muslim, he's a doctor, I think, in the university. And then he says that this is against Islamic law. There's no such thing as marriage at the age of nine. This is against Islamic law. The people who say this are insulting the prophet, are attacking the prophet. This doesn't exist in our tradition. So, essentially, he's making it seem like all the scholars in Islamic history — which is the reality, the overwhelming majority of 99% of them who accepted and believed in this report — they essentially were evil people then. If this doesn't exist in Islam, then none of them knew Islamic law. And they were people who are accusing the prophet of Islam of something wasn't appropriate, because he keeps saying the prophet was compassionate and all that, which we believe in.", "Some people can make this claim. There's people in Islamic history that said that she was nine, she was 18, or she was 17, or whatever. And nobody's going to attack them. Nobody's going to say anything. If you say I believe she was this, I'm not going to make a video unnecessarily comment to you. But when you don't only say this, but you say anyone who believes other than this, this is against Islamic law, and those people are attacking the prophet, and make it seem like you're defending the honor of the prophet — which essentially the rest of the Muslims, all of them, believe opposite to what you're saying. It's an issue when you have the Muslims actually believing this and you claim that this is not part of Islamic law. And the other reason I wanted to also respond is because he actually provides a lot of what he claims as historical proofs, evidences from the Hadith, the Quran, historical sources.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Was Aisha 18 Years Old When She Married The Prophet? | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5lzZclwwqDg", ((23, "0:23–1:09"), (73, "1:13–2:02"), (125, "2:05–3:06"),), id: 2214)
