#passage(("Let's use common sense. Do you know the age of marriage 200 years ago in the UK? We're not talking 1,400 years ago. 7 years [old]. You know who says that? William Blackstone. He was a jurist who lived in that time. Sir William Blackstone is being taught in Oxford and Cambridge in law universities, because Sir William Blackstone has a book called Commentaries on the British Law of England. So he comments on the British law at the time, and then he says in his book, page 110, a woman can be given in marriage at the age of seven, entitled to dowry ([dowry] is where you give a marital gift) at the age of nine.", "But that was not only the UK. America: it was 10 years old, 200 years ago; in Delaware it was seven. So that's not something new. That was human history, and that only changed recently.", "That time, today's life of living a life of disobedience to God and hedonistic life of having intercourse was not the case. Remember your country and other Christian countries, where intercourse before marriage was wrong according to the Bible, and Judaism: intercourse before marriage is wrong. So since intercourse before marriage is wrong, people had only marriage. So when you say age of consent, that's a new modern concept that comes from liberalism. Before liberalism people actually believed in marriage. They believed: okay, if you want to be with a woman, you actually get married to that woman. So there's no such thing as the age of consent in the past. It's just the age of marriage.", "That's a very funny thing, because today they say the age of consent can be 14, 15, 16, but the age of marriage is only 18. So I can have intercourse with a girl, have a baby with her, but I can't marry her. Why? Where's the common sense there? Where's the logic there? So if you look at history, age of marriage was very low. When did it start to increase? After the industrial revolution. They wanted to introduce people into the workforce. They had this system of schools.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Atheist Shocked By Muslim After Questioning The Prophet's Marriage! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SxG2d79W6j0", ((29, "0:29–1:32"), (102, "1:42–1:58"), (122, "2:02–2:49"), (170, "2:50–3:18"),), id: 2219)
// U ML138:213-280 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("The age of marriage in the UK was 7 years old in the 1700s and 1800s. The fact is, that was the case throughout human history until only recently, let's say the end or the middle 1900s, where you had the 2nd World War, where they introduced women into the workforce, the liberal \"enlightenment\" movement, feminism, all of these things. Until all of that, the age was this low in the whole world.", "You probably think about a number, 18. That's the only number you probably think about in your mind right now for being OK or not being OK, but that's because it's what your society convinced you. But the age of consent today in almost the majority of the world is 15 to 16. In the US today, it depends on the state: there are states that have as low as 15, some 16. So it's even today, in the West and in the entire world. Like the Philippines and Japan or wherever, they had it 12 and 13 only 10 years ago or something like that; they only recently changed it.", "The people in the West are living in a bubble, and they think the whole world is operating under their bubble. They're completely not in a view of human history, not only how humans lived history, but even today, they're still living history! They are completely unaware of that, and they would come and talk about the Prophet Muhammad and age this, age that. The world does not operate under what you've been taught in school or in your societal narrative. Just because someone convinced you something is a no-no, it doesn't mean that the rest of the world operates under that same idea, or that the whole human history operates under that idea.", "That's why throughout human history, until 100 years ago, you're not going to find a single person that criticised the Prophet Muhammad for that. You're going to see people criticising Prophet Muhammad for everything: for wars, battles and all of these stuff. But you're not going to find a single person, non-Muslim, who criticised the Prophet Muhammad for this action. Now, first and foremost, you're not going to find anyone in his society criticising him for that, because 'Aisha, his wife, was betrothed already before him. Before the Prophet was introduced or even in the picture, she was already betrothed to someone else. Betrothal is like engagement. This is how society was.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Confronts Muslim On The Marriage Of The Prophet! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vz1jgBpf8UE", ((752, "12:32–12:56"), (781, "13:01–13:33"), (817, "13:37–14:14"), (889, "14:49–15:37"),), id: 2220)
