#passage(("The thing is, people think it's a strong argument because of how society is today. It's not. If you study history, you would realize you're going against all the entire human history, cuz everybody was marrying young. The Queen Elizabeth and Richard the Second: she was 6 years old when she married him. Victoria was 15 or something, I think, when she was ruling the country. Things were different. We're talking about 100, 200 years ago.", "You cannot use today's lens of the kids, because back in the day what would happen is you would have the girl reach puberty, she's ready for marriage. There is no elementary school, secondary school, as we have today. There was no school people go. The girl is ready: she reached puberty means she's ready to have kids. She's ready to get married and she starts a family.", "We also had low life expectancy. There's a person called Imam Shafi. In Islamic history, he's, let's say, 200 years, 300 years after the prophet. He says he saw a grandmother at the age of 21. That means she must have married at the age of nine or something. The society is completely different.", "Today, the society changed and they started changing the numbers. From 14 you go to 16. Every country keeps changing. Go just check in the last 10 years how countries change their ages. Who cares about what you change? The nature that God has created: once the girl goes through puberty, she's able to have birth. If you don't do harm to her, then on what basis are you claiming you cannot have marriage?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Hate Preacher Attempts To Disrupt Civil Muslim-Christian Dialogue! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=X05zRHM4kVs", ((1918, "31:58–32:19"), (1941, "32:21–32:40"), (1960, "32:40–33:03"), (1997, "33:17–33:39"),), id: 2222)
