#passage(("Sir William Blackstone was a jurist. He says a woman can be given in marriage at the age of 7 and entitled to dowry. He’s talking about the state law, he’s not talking about his opinion. He's talking about the law in England at the time. This is what was being practiced in the country.", "So, point is this: we don’t apply fallacies. Presentism: applying the values of today. The young woman of today, their mind, their upbringing, their social life, how long do they live, their life expectancy, and we compare it to the past and how things were operating 1400 years ago in the deserts. That is a fallacy called presentism, which is to apply the morals of today onto the past.", "And if you look at the life expectancy in the past, according to Western historians, they'll tell you it was 30 to 35 years old or something at that time. If this was the life expectancy, let’s say they get married at 18. She gives birth at 20, let's say, and then, 10 years old, the parents die. Can a society like this continue? In actuality, no. It will never continue. That’s why you'll never find anyone in the past ever having an issue with the marriage of the Prophet ﷺ. It was well known, but no one criticised him for this.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Muslim Questioned About The Prophet's Marriage! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lzXN6Mv9k8A", ((202, "3:22–3:57"), (241, "4:01–4:24"), (266, "4:26–5:04"),), id: 2223)
