#passage(("Of course I don't have any issues with this doctor. This is not to attack him. But he's brought what he says is evidences.", "The first claim he made is he claims that this is only one hadith. It's the only hadith they use, and we don't look at any other Hadith narrations, no other reports mentioned about Aisha. Now, how true is this idea? This claim is not new. It's not a new claim. This claim is mentioned by a different person before: the claim about the report, talking about the authenticity of the report, and there's only one person and this person has an issue. And the person they talk about is Hisham.", "But first he says this Hadith comes from Aisha. It's not going to be attributed to the prophet, because it's not an action. She's not talking about the prophet; she's talking about herself. So the Hadith is attributed to her; she's talking about herself. It's evidence for us. So yes, this Hadith is attributed to a companion. In this case it's Aisha speaking herself about her own age. So it is not an issue at all.", "Now the issue that they always bring up about this narration: they say there's a reporter called Hisham [ibn Urwah]. And they try to criticize this narrator, and they say we only have this narration coming only from Hisham [ibn Urwah]. It's only one person, it's only one Hadith, like he claims. But the reality is that is absolutely false. This narration comes from Hisham. Who's Hisham? He's the son of al-Zubayr. Who's al-Zubayr? He's the relative of Aisha. Of course he's going to know about her, because she's his aunt. So al-Zubayr is going to know about the age of Aisha when he reports the report. Hisham is his son. Everyone agrees that al-Zubayr is a reliable person.", "Now they talk about Hisham maybe making some mistakes. Of course this idea first is refuted. Him making mistakes when he got older or whatever is refuted by al-Dhahabi himself: he says that he was reliable, and he refutes the claim of the people who are trying to say that he became weak when he got older. No, he didn't. But the claim is what? They're saying this is only a report from Hisham ibn Urwah. So, let's accept this idea: maybe Hisham ibn Urwah made some mistakes when he got older. So he's reliable and maybe he made some mistakes. But it's not only reported by Hisham ibn Urwah.", "Who else reports this narration from Aisha radi Allahu anha? Al-Zuhri reports this narration from Urwah directly. So, now we don't have Hisham, who they claim had issues with his memory. Now, we have al-Zuhri reporting from Urwah from Aisha, and that is another hadith now, in a different route, in Sahih Muslim. Is that it? No, we have another route where you have al-Aswad: Amash reports from Ibrahim from al-Aswad from Aisha. So, you have another person, al-Aswad, reports from Aisha, and it's also in Muslim. Then we have a different route: from Muhammad ibn Amr from Yahya ibn Abdur Rahman from Aisha radi Allahu anha, and this is in Abu Dawud. It's not even Muslim or Bukhari now. It's authentic. So, to claim that this comes from one route is insane.", "So who else other than Hisham reports it from Urwah ibn Zubair? Because they claim that it's Hisham, and Hisham may have made a mistake. Who else reports it? Al-Aswad, Qasim ibn Abdur Rahman, Qasim ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr, Umrah bint Abdur Rahman, Yahya ibn Abdur Rahman ibn Hatib, and Muhammad ibn Qasim ibn Abi Bakr; she's also his aunt. So, he's another person reporting from Urwah, and he's also the relative of Aisha. So, of course he knows about his aunt. He's reported directly from her.", "And from Hisham ibn Urwah, we have around 18 different narrators narrating from Hisham ibn Urwah. You have Ibn Shihab al-Zuhri, you have Abu Hamza Maymun, and, by the way, these [reporters] are from different regions. And you have narrators of this hadith reporting from Hisham in Medina, to show that he didn't only narrate this narration in Mecca; they also narrated it in Medina. So you have Abu Zinad reporting from him, Abdullah ibn Dhakwan; you have his son Abdur Rahman ibn Abi Zinad, the son of Abi Zinad; and you have Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya. They all report from him, and they're from Medina. And from the people of Mecca, you have Sufyan ibn Uyaynah; we're talking about giants now of knowledge. You have Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid. And from the people of Basra, Iraq, you have Hammad ibn Salamah, Hammad ibn Zayd, Wuhaib ibn Khalid. And I can go on and on. All of these people are reporting from Hisham.", "So you have 18 people reporting from Hisham, great scholars of hadith. If this narration had an issue, they would all criticize, they would all weaken this narration. So the reason scholars like Bukhari and Muslim included this narration is because you have all of these routes in all of these different cities. It's mass reported from him. But it's not only from Hisham. Like I said, remove Hisham from the equation. You still have al-Aswad, al-Qasim ibn Abdur Rahman, you have Amrah bint Abdur Rahman, Yahya ibn Abdur Rahman. I'll go on and on and on. So there is so many others that narrate this narration, and they narrate it directly without going through Hisham ibn Urwah.", "But does it even stop there? Because he says we don't look at other ahadith narrated. So you have another hadith in Imam Ahmad; it's not even from Aisha herself. Some people say that maybe Aisha didn't know her age. You have other people reporting, not Aisha; they're not reporting from Aisha, they're other people reporting the narration. They're reporting the incident. We have this narration in Musnad of Imam Ahmad. So we have Abu Salamah. He's one of the seven fuqaha of Medina, great scholars of Medina. You also have Yahya ibn Abdur-Rahman ibn Hatib also in this narration in Musnad Ahmad. You have two people here: two authentic, reliable people, from the greatest tabi'in, reporting. And they don't report from Aisha in this narration. They don't say they heard Aisha. They report an incident where it mentions the age of Aisha, and it's in Musnad Ahmad.", "So, is it really that it is one narration, one hadith, we don't look at the others, or is it the fact that the person who is saying this is the one who isn't doing that? Why do you think Bukhari and Muslim and others included these narrations? Bukhari would include it in his book because he knows that this is mass transmitted. So, when we talk about science of hadith, we're not just talking like this. We have evidences for the things that we say. When Bukhari says this is authentic, there is evidences to support that this is authentic, that we can demonstrate with names and chains and everything. When people criticize things, you need to criticize them based on knowledge. You can't say there's only one hadith about this, and that's not true.", "Now, the second claim, he says, \"Let's analyze this from a historical point of view.\" He mentions first an individual who died in 1948. When we talk about scholars and we talk about people of knowledge, we talk about historical realities, you don't go to someone who lived 70, 80 years ago, and we're talking about an incident that happened 1,400 years ago. These are not sources. Sources are primary source materials. This individual, by the way, is a philosopher. And he had this book called Hatun, where he tried to advocate for equality, complete equality between the genders, feminism essentially. He says the niqab is regressive and it hinders the progress of the ummah. He was one of those progressive people, revisionists of the religion. Because this was after the fall of the Ottoman Empire, you have all of these revisionists trying to change things and say, \"Look, the ummah regressed because of this.\" You have all of these people pushing this idea. So, when we talk about historical sources, you don't go and bring someone in the 20th century who wrote a book, who was a progressive person, who was a philosopher, and we say that he's going to give us information.", "And then he goes on and he mentioned another philosopher and a poet from India this time. You see the pattern? What is the pattern happening here? You have the downfall of the Ottoman Empire, you have the secularization of Islam, you have the modernization taking place, all of this stuff. You have individuals in this period, which is the second person he mentioned. He died in 1914. Another person was 100 years ago or so. These are not primary source materials. One guy talks about this tradition, the Arabs had this tradition. You can say Arabs had this tradition, that tradition. That's not proof for anything. He even himself knows that it's not proof. And then you mention another individual who says this was her age. We say he died 1914, but this book was written in the end of his life, because he didn't finish it. He only got to write two volumes of it. And this was published in [1918] and 1920, I believe. These are not evidences.", "Now, I want you to understand this idea, very very important. Do you know the people who said that the age of Aisha was nine? You're going to put aside Ibn Hazm. You're going to put aside Ibn Abd al-Barr. You're going to put aside Ibn Hisham. All of these people died in different ages. You're going to put aside Ibn Sa'd. You're going to put aside Ibn Kathir. You're going to put aside al-Tabari. You're going to put aside Ibn Hajar al-Asqalani. You're going to put aside al-Dhahabi. You're going to put aside Imam Ahmad. You're going to put aside al-Shafi'i. You're going to put aside Bukhari. You're going to put aside Muslim. All of those people. You're going to put aside Imam al-Nawawi. I can go on and on and on mentioning scholars and people of knowledge, all of them mentioning that Aisha was at the age of nine. This was our Islamic history. Some people think today we are going to be more knowledgeable than these scholars of 1,400 years ago, that know the reports, or closer to the event, had exact reports reaching them with chains of narration, because 100 years ago and 80 years ago or 70 years ago someone said something. So, it's very important. When someone uses these words and says, \"This is evidence,\" we got to be a bit more critical with this idea. What do you mean by evidence? How is this an evidence when it is something that has been mentioned all this time ago?", "Now, the next claim he makes, he cites things about Asma: she was 27 years. We don't have a problem with this idea. But then he says Asma was older than Aisha. And then he cites any sources? Nope. No sources mentioned. What's the source? This idea that you're saying, that Asma was 10 years older than Aisha: what are the sources? I don't have a problem accepting this idea that Asma was, let's say, 27 when the migration happened. But where is this evidence? What is the source that she was 10 years older than Aisha? The reality is this proof comes from an individual called Abdur Rahman ibn Abi Zinad. He's weakened by Imam Ahmad as a scholar. He's weakened by Ali ibn Madini. He's weakened by Nisai. He's weakened by ibn Adi. He's weakened by Yahya ibn Ma'in. And I can go on and on. So, this idea, which is mentioned, I believe, in two sources or so, or a few sources, is tracing back to this individual. And this individual has been weakened by scholars. So, you don't have a source. That's why he didn't cite any sources. He didn't even cite a source for this idea, for the difference in age, because the whole thing now is based on this idea that they have this difference in 10 years, but he didn't give any sources.", "Some people try to use another type of evidence, which is that Aisha was 5 years younger than Fatima. And they quote ibn Hajar saying this. And then they say that Fatima was born, the Prophet was 35 years old that time. When the revelation came down, essentially, this is when Aisha was born, according to this understanding. But the reality is this is absolutely unfounded. There's no evidence for this. There's a narration that traces back to someone called Al-Waqidi. Al-Waqidi is a agreed-upon liar. Imam Ahmad says he's a liar. I can go on and on and mention. This is not a contested issue, that Al-Waqidi is a liar.", "Then you have Al-Dhahabi. What does Al-Dhahabi say about the difference between age? Al-Dhahabi tells you that actually the difference between Asma and Aisha is from 13 to 19, you can say. So if we say the difference in age between Asma and Aisha, that Asma is 17 years older than Aisha or 18 years older than Aisha, no issues, if she was 27 at the time of migration and Aisha was around 10 or 9. There is no proof for this idea, which he did not provide any source for: the difference in age between Aisha and Asma.", "Now, the fourth claim he makes is the sabiqun, and this claim is a bit strange, because honestly, this claim is actually against him. Because if Aisha is born in Mecca, of course, we believe that Aisha was born in Mecca and she was born with the believers. And then he says sabiqun is referring to the first three or four years after revelation, from 610 to 614 or whatever, and Aisha is mentioned among them; that would make no sense. Allah in the Quran tells us who are the sabiqun: of the Muhajirun and Ansar. Allah says the sabiqun al-awwalun of the Muhajirun and Ansar, those who did migration and those who aided the Prophet Sallallahu Alaihi Wasallam. So, it's not those who were in the first three or four years of revelation. It's those who were believers and migrated to the Prophet. And Aisha, whether she was from those who migrated or even if she was in Medina, she would be from the sabiqun. But she's mentioned with the sabiqun because she migrated with her father. So, to say the sabiqun means this, when the Quran literally explicitly says that the sabiqun are the Muhajirun and Ansar, it makes no sense.", "Now, the fifth claim he makes, he mentions Imam Nawawi. Why am I smiling? Because Imam Nawawi himself says that Aisha was 9 years old at the age of marriage. So, how do you mention Imam Nawawi and say he's the person that all the Muslims are going to agree on, if Imam Nawawi is against your position? How are you going to mention him and use them as evidence? For me, it's really strange. And remember, we don't have an issue with the claim that he just made quoting Imam Nawawi, which affirms that Asma was 27. But this claim now is based on the third claim he made, which is that Asma is 10 years older than Aisha. But he didn't provide any proof. Honestly, do you think Imam Nawawi is going to make this statement, and he's going to say that she was nine, at the same time he knows that Asma was 27, and he's not going to say, \"Oh, actually, no, she was 18\"? Essentially, you praise Imam Nawawi, but in the same light you're kind of saying that you're smarter than him, because today, in this day and age, you've realized her real age, while he didn't know her real age, while you're quoting his report. You got to internalize this idea a little bit. These are the proofs that he's providing, saying that these are proofs for his position. [It's] definitely evidence against his position.", "Now, the next claim he makes is about Surah Al-Qamar. He quotes Surah Al-Qamar, saying, \"The hour is their appointed time, and the hour will be the most catastrophic and most bitter.\" And he mentions this verse, and he says that Aisha radiyallahu anha reported this verse and she said that this verse was revealed when I was a girl and I was playing. He says she was playing with toys and that she remembers the events. I don't want to use the word lie, cuz I'm talking with a Muslim, but the Hadith doesn't even mention anything about toys. It doesn't mention anything about playing with toys. She says, \"I was a jariya al'ab.\" She just states a fact. She was a young girl playing when this verse was revealed. He misquoted the Hadith. That's the first thing. The second thing: he says she remembers the event. She didn't say she's remembering the event. She said this happened when I was this age. It's like me saying the war in Iraq happened when I was 2 years old. Does it mean I'm saying when I was 2 years old I remember the war? No. This is just a hypothetical date, a hypothetical event. She's stating a fact. Absolutely no issues with that.", "Then he says Surah al-Qamar was revealed in the year 614. And he cites no source again. And this is my issue with this video: when you make claims and you cite no sources, but he's presenting the video as if he's giving you sources and evidences. But in reality, he hasn't presented any sources for this. And again, even if we accept this reality, we don't believe that all the whole chapters were revealed together. Different verses are revealed at different times. But the overwhelming majority of scholars, the overwhelming majority of the ulama, say that Surah al-Qamar was revealed 4 years before the Hijrah to Mecca, around 8 AH or so. So where's the issue, if she was three, four years old when this verse was revealed? There is no conclusive evidence for when the chapter was revealed. I'm saying, if we want to take the overall majority scholar position, we don't have any issues with that. But this idea of 614, what is the source?", "Even if you say that she was just a young baby, because they're saying, \"Oh, no, it's impossible that she was just a young baby,\" whether you say she was just a young baby or this or that, that doesn't go against the hadith, because the word used in the hadith is jariya. Wa anna jariya al'ab. Jariya in the Arabic language is used for suckling babies, still suckling babies that don't eat food. In the hadith of the Prophet Sallallahu Alaihi Wasallam, he says, yunzahu bawlul jariya: that the urine of the baby that didn't eat food yet, still suckling, you can spray water on it to purify it. And the word used is jariya, for a suckling infant, little baby. So when she uses the word jariya playing, she can be a little baby playing. It's okay. She can be, even if we take the opinion of the majority scholars, three or four years old playing. A young girl. No issues with this at all. But the issue is where you add things to the hadith. And then you bring no source and make this evidence, saying that it was the year 614. It wasn't the year 614.", "Now, the seventh claim he makes, which is something we use, we don't have any issues with that: that Aisha was betrothed before the prophet of Islam, because it supports us. This was in the tradition of the Arabs. The idea of young marriages, like I showed historically, was a thing that happened. It wasn't something strange. So, he says she was betrothed or engaged to Jubair ibn Mut'im before the prophet of Islam, and she was so young, and no culture would allow this. No, that's not true. Because this was a promise: Mut'im ibn Adi, the father of Jubair ibn Mut'im, and Abu Bakr, the father of Aisha, made promises to each other. That's it. That was in the culture of the Arabs. Engagement there doesn't mean you're going to sit together, like today people think engagement: I'm going to give you a ring and we go together to the restaurant. No. We have this idea, this promise: I'm going to promise my son for your daughter, even if they're babies. I have a friend and he has a baby, let's say, and I have a son or whatever. I'm going to say, \"Okay, let's make a promise between us that I'm going to marry my daughter to your son, because they're the same age or something.\" Or he's 2 years younger or something. Just promises. None of this is actually anything happening. There's no issues with this. You can make a marriage contract at any age in Islam. There's no physical interaction happening. There's no consummation. So, of course, nobody's going to criticize this, and this was a part of the culture.", "He talks about the migration to Habash, and then he mentions that she was mentioning that this was very early on and she wasn't born yet, and she's remembering it and saying it. Her father told her about it. Why are you saying it like she needs to be there to report an event? You don't need to be present in the place to report an event. That's her father, so her father can tell her what happened in that time. And then Aisha can report to us what happened. We have Sahaba reporting from the Prophet incidents that they were not in, which they heard from other Sahaba. You reporting an incident does not mean you were present there in the incident yourself, existing at that time. She could be mentioning a part of the narration which she was there, and then she could be remembering something that her father told, which is another part of the narration.", "But this narration is actually evidence against them. Why? Because the beginning of the narration, she says, I came to recognition: the age where you can recognize things. When you're still a baby suckling, you don't recognize anything, but then, let's say you're three, four, and then you recognize things around you. She says when I started recognizing things, my father and mother were already Muslims. But according to them, she is born before Islam. Five years before Islam is the claim they're making to make this calculation work. So if she was around four years old, there was still no [revelation] yet. Her father and mother were not Muslims. How is she saying when I came to recognize things they were already Muslims? So this narration is evidence against you.", "Now, I think this is one of the strangest claims he's making. He says Aisha died at the age 74. Again, no sources. Absolutely no sources. And we have narrations that Aisha, at the death of the prophet, was 18, which would only make sense if she was nine after the marriage, after the migration. They conveniently ignore this report as well, and ignore this piece of information. But now they're talking about the death of Aisha herself: she died at the age of 74. There is no source for this. I don't believe there's a single reliable Islamic scholar in Islamic history that mentioned this. I haven't found any myself. I tried to find; no sources. None. Islamic scholars would say she was around the age 66-67. They all mention this. This is the overwhelming majority view, that she was around this age, or younger, they would say. But this idea that she was 74, we don't have people taking this position. They added essentially eight years to the 66, so they can make it 74, so then you can add eight years to the nine and she was 17. And this is the thing: you can try and defend something, but you can't make up information about Islamic history to support your beliefs.", "Now, the next claim he makes is about the Battle of Uhud. He says the prophet did not allow younger Sahaba to participate in the battle until they reach this age. And how would he allow Aisha to come? The prophet, when he went to the battles, like Uhud or any of the other battles, he would bring one of his wives with him. They would have this lottery kind of thing, and then whoever gets selected will go with the prophet. So, the prophet went to the battle and he took his wife with him, Aisha. And she was 11, and she was bringing water, because she's the wife of the prophet, and she's present because she is with the prophet of Allah. And she's bringing water to the people in the battlefield. How's that a problem? She's not going to go fight with the army. Prophet is stopping soldiers in a younger age. He's not going to stop Aisha from bringing a little bit of water when she's 11 years old. Let's make this distinction very clear. So, that's not an evidence really in any way, shape or form.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Was Aisha 18 Years Old When She Married The Prophet? | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5lzZclwwqDg", ((884, "14:44–14:51"), (913, "15:13–15:47"), (948, "15:48–16:18"), (979, "16:19–17:01"), (1021, "17:01–17:40"), (1061, "17:41–18:35"), (1117, "18:37–19:06"), (1146, "19:06–19:56"), (1197, "19:57–20:37"), (1238, "