#passage(("Now, to talk about the battles, you said 80 to 100. What is the source for this funny story about 80 to 100 battles? That is obviously a joke that never happened. Of course, they were not 80 to 100 battles. You can say from 10 to 20 battles, if you even want to go to an extreme, if you want to consider certain things battles and other not. But they're definitely not 80 to 100.", "Now, these battles, the overwhelming majority of them were DEFENSIVE battles. So the first battle, for example, the Battle of Badr, was a defensive battle. The Prophet left Mecca because they were trying to kill him and his Companions, and he had to go to Medina. They followed him and they fought him there and tried to kill him. The Battle of alKhandaq or alAhzab, it was also a defensive battle. The Battle of Uhud was also a defensive battle. There were so many defensive battles.", "Even certain battles, which you can say are not necessarily defensive, they were done for justified reasons. For example, there was a caravan going with the people of Makkah, and the Prophet said that this caravan should be raided. This caravan is carrying goods. The Muslims who went to Medina left all their goods there. So they had a right to reclaim the things that they left, because they were leaving for their lives. They had no choice, because they were going to kill them. So now there's a caravan with goods. So, they're trying to get back what belongs to them. So that is a justified reason. Similarly, with the Conquest of Makkah: coming back to Makkah, their homeland, and their families and their city.", "And when the Prophetﷺ came in the Conquest of Makkah with a huge massive army, and the people in Makkah, who were fighting all of these battles, trying to kill him one time after the other, you know what the Prophet did when he came and they surrendered? They were persecuting him for 13 years. They killed many of his Companions. He had to leave because they were going to kill him. They sent a bounty hunter after him when he left. Now, do you know what the Prophet did? He killed them all? No! Of course he didn't kill them all. But that's the logical explanation. And even if he did that, nobody would blame him. But do you know what he did? He let them go free! He let the people who persecuted him, killed some of his Companions, plotted to kill him, sent a bounty hunter after him when he left, he let them go free.", "Now, you cannot do this! I cannot do this! We wouldn't do that! Someone who was persecuting me for 13 years, killed my family members, I had to leave because they were plotting to kill me, they fought battles after battles trying to kill me, they didn't even stop when I left. But when I came back, when I was in a position of power, I showed MERCY. Now, that is the Prophet Muhammad.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Confronts Muslim On The Marriage Of The Prophet! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vz1jgBpf8UE", ((1282, "21:22–22:17"), (1339, "22:19–22:46"), (1368, "22:48–23:22"), (1406, "23:26–24:09"), (1452, "24:12–24:31"),), id: 2279)
