#passage(("Reason number ten: Muhammad's first impression of his revelations was that they were demonic in origin.", "First of all, the report isn't even authentic. It's narrated by [Ubayd ibn Umayr]; he's not even an eyewitness, so we could just drop it like that. This is David Wood's 10th strongest argument against Islam, and the hadith isn't even authentic. Secondly, the report doesn't say demon [possessed], and the common used term majnoon means madman, crazy. And naturally, Rasulullah going to town and telling people \"I saw an angel\", people are going to think that he is [a madman]. The same word, mejnun, is used by the Pharaoh to refer to Moses, by the people of Abraham to refer to Abraham, by the people of Noah referring to Noah, and they're not referring to them as people have been possessed; they're referring to them as people that are mad, that they're crazy, because Noah is building an ark on land. So, subhan allah, this isn't really much of an argument.", "It's just unbelievably silly. You will never hear the Jews use these type of arguments against the Muslims; it always has to be mainly Protestant Christians, because they're the most illiterate when it comes to the scriptures. They do not have a clue about the Semitic background of the scriptures that they hold in their hands every single day.",), "Farid Responds, “David Wood's Top Ten Arguments Debunked!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YPvZ_xNePzo", ((54, "0:54–1:02"), (120, "2:00–2:58"), (271, "4:31–4:52"),), id: 2304)
