#passage(("First thing is very simple stuff, but one needs to have proper reading comprehension in order to understand what's being said in these reports. All one needs to do is read the text slowly and try to understand what the eyewitness is trying to say. Now unfortunately, if we approach a text with some preconceptions, we may be spinning the text, twisting it in a way that conflicts with the intentions of the original narrator.", "For example, in this clip we see Dr [Jalaluddeen], a wannabe neurologist, making the claim that the prophet peace be upon him used to fall into his own lap, and apparently that's a sign of epilepsy. The text actually says [Anas] said that the prophet peace be upon him had his head on his lap, then the revelation came to him, and [Anas] said: I felt that my lap was going to split. In other words, what we find here is [Anas] is referring to his own lap. And by the way, how do you even put your head in your own lap? Is that even physically possible?", "Now here is another example. The people saw fire, a light, in the graveyard, and they went there. They found that the messenger of Allah peace be upon him was in a grave, and he was saying: give me your companion. Now where in the report does it say that he was speaking to the dead man? Was he telling the dead man \"give me your companion\", or was he telling those that are with him \"give me your companion\"? It doesn't take much intelligence to come to the conclusion that the prophet peace be upon him was actually telling the sahaba, the people around him, his companions, \"give me your companions\", so that I could bury him in the grave. There's nothing in the report about the prophet peace be upon him speaking to this dead man.", "Now I could be mistaken, but it really seems like these two individuals sincerely came to this understanding; their reactions seem genuine. So it is possible that these are two of the dumbest [mortals] in the world, or they have their biases, which is unfortunately causing them to comprehend reports in ways that aren't supposed to be understood. Unfortunately, now even the most mundane report is going to be evidence of the prophet peace be upon him acting in a crazy manner. Unfortunately this is a trend with a lot of critics of Islam: they have a hard time putting their biases on the side and dealing with the reports as they were meant to be understood.", "A very important aspect of dealing with the text has to do with understanding the classical definitions of terms. Again, we're dealing with 7th century Arabic; we're not dealing with modern Arabic. This is why it's so important to refer to classical dictionaries when it comes to understanding the meanings of terms. Now this is something that may seem to be a bit too complicated, but I've actually seen non-Arabs that can speak Arabic actually use lexicons in order to come to the correct definitions of terms. So it's something that can be done. It's not easy, but it [can] be done.", "Now in some cases words will be twisted by critics of Islam in order for them to come to a result that they'd like. A red flag shoots up the moment that they translate a word that conflicts with other translations found in texts that were translated by Muslims. Often they will claim that the translation is incorrect, and they will attempt to show you something. I've never seen anyone claim that the term means pain. Now personally I have no idea what this is based on, this information that they've dug up from this website. More importantly, why would I care about a website that's not referencing its material? I don't know who worked on the website, I don't know who put this data in. This website is just a website. Is a lexicographer working on this website? Is the linguist? I don't know who's putting this information in there.", "Now if we return to classical dictionaries, we don't find them using the word [karb] this way; it's never used to be referring to pain. Even a modern dictionary like Lane's Lexicon doesn't refer to [karb] as pain. Now Dr [Jalaluddeen] is someone that loves to use Lane's Lexicon; however, when he did not find the term [karb] meaning pain in Lane's Lexicon, he switched over to a modern dictionary in order to find someone somewhere that referred to a word in the way that he wanted it to be referred to. In any case, here are some scans from some classical Arabic dictionaries, and as you can see, the term [karb] is never referring to [alam] or pain.", "Now the term [paroxysm], or any Arabic equivalent, cannot be found in classical lexicons that Lane is quoting. We find the same in the other sources that Lane is referring to. So where did [paroxysm] come from? It is Lane's interpretation of the term. Then Dr [Jalaluddeen] used the English dictionary to link paroxysm with convulsions, and because of this, every time the word shows up in the hadith, he's claiming that this means convulsion. My response to this, again, simply: in classical dictionaries the term never is associated with convulsion.", "Context is everything, and when it comes to apologetics, of course, it's not an exception. Often evidence that is used against you will be clarified by the same report that is being used against you. It is something that you should keep in mind: people will use reports against you that in reality will be evidence for you. All you gotta do is return to the report and read it for yourself. There are so many examples of stuff like this happening.", "He is arguing that this is proof that the prophet peace be upon him had epilepsy, but now let's read the rest of the report from earlier on in the page to get a clear idea of what is going on. It says: When we had turned to the prophet at dawn, and while we were with him seated, we mentioned those who were killed among the Muslims, until the messenger of God said: Come with us. So we went with him, and we were twenty men, until we finally reached al-assaf. [Jabir] said: Food sufficient for one or two men was brought. The messenger of God put his hand in it and said: Take in the name of God. We ate from it until we were full, and by God it did not appear to us that we had touched a thing. Then they brought us ripe dates in a saucer from the first fruit or a little later, and the messenger of God said: In the name of God, eat. He said: We ate until we were full, and I looked in the saucer, and there remained approximately what was brought in it.", "So as you can see, in this report a miracle is being mentioned, when food is literally being multiplied, and a basket of food is sufficient for 20 people. Not only that, but the amount of food didn't even lessen. Oh, and by the way, I don't even accept this report is authentic, because the author of the book himself is not reliable. Remember authority: yes, I will actually reject evidence that's for Islam if there is an issue with the source, and that's because I'm someone who's consistent. These people will actually use a report against Islam even if it includes a miracle.", "Now here's another example. Check this guy out: he's even authenticating the report. No, he's not authenticating the report; he's actually weakening the report due to the anonymity of one of the narrators. So that's how, by looking at the context of the grading, we can actually arrive at what the author is trying to say. But now let's examine the actual text. The report states that [Ali] is at home when the prophet peace be upon him asks him if he had prayed. He said: No, O messenger of Allah. So he supplicated to Allah, and the [sun] came back. He said: I saw the sun come out after it had [set], so that I could pray [Asr], the afternoon prayer. Now this individual, doctor [Jalaluddeen], actually wants you to accept this report because it has evidence for epilepsy. Yes, he actually wants you to accept the [sun] returned. He's actually trying to sell you a supernatural hadith in order for you to accept his evidence for epilepsy. You see how inconsistent these people are, just in order to score some points.",), "Farid Responds, “Refutations 101 [Crash Course]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CgfiwsD4Sns", ((1101, "18:21–18:50"), (1130, "18:50–19:50"), (1190, "19:50–21:28"), (1288, "21:28–22:16"), (1343, "22:23–23:03"), (1383, "23:03–24:56"), (1496, "24:56–25:35"), (1619, "26:59–27:46"), (1699, "28:19–28:47"), (1766, "29:26–30:28"), (1831, "30:31–31:12"), (1872, "31:12–32:41"),), id: 2314)
