#passage(("We don't find anything in the report mentioning anything about teeth or the jittering of the teeth, and yet he interprets the report that has nothing to do with that. The report itself is supposed to suggest that this is a miraculous event. Omar is saying: whenever a revelation occurs, we can hear a sound coming from him, and it sounds like bees. So Gondal is turning that into a symptom for epilepsy.", "So I'm like, okay, he's actually going to bring 10 hadiths to prove his point. 10, that's quite a large number. Remember, at the moment that's just a claim. Let's go through the evidence.", "It is narrated by Muhammad bin, who is accused of lying by multiple classical scholars, who narrates it from Imran, who is an unknown narrator, who narrates it from Imran, who is reliable, but he's just simply not an eyewitness to the event. So basically we have three issues with this report that [Jalaluddeen] is trying to claim is authentic. But didn't [Jalaluddeen] just say that all the narrators are reliable? Well, that's because [Jalaluddeen] doesn't know how to read footnotes. Now, this is a type of sloppiness that we should be getting used to.", "So, hadith number two is actually hadith number one. Just because the book is [Urdu] doesn't mean that it's a second hadith. Remember the report by [Ibn Sa'd]? Well, this is the exact same report, same narrators.", "This time, there are major issues with this one. The first is that HB is unknown in status. Also, his mother is unknown. And perhaps the clearest problem is that the event mentioned in this hadith occurs in Medina, and the verses mentioned in this hadith were actually revealed in Mecca. So the report pretty much conflicts the [well-established] chronology of events. By the way, there's nothing here about teeth chattering. It's about jaw trembling. There is a difference.", "Once again, Dr. [Jalaluddeen] repeats the same hadith as a previous one. And guess what? That's it. Those are the 10 hadiths that he claimed to have found. He provides one fabricated report, repeats it twice, once in [Urdu], then he brings another baseless hadith about beard shaking or jaw trembling, and then he repeats it again from another secondary source. So what he really did find was one hadith, and he just repeated it three times.",), "Farid Responds, “The Most Desperate ex-Muslim”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vuN8BwMnzL8", ((32, "0:32–1:12"), (86, "1:26–1:45"), (154, "2:34–3:20"), (219, "3:39–4:15"), (287, "4:47–5:12"), (327, "5:27–6:01"),), id: 2315)
