#passage(("Firstly, the word used is, according to dictionaries, the sound that one makes when one is sleeping. So that can either be a heavy breathing, or it could be snoring and not snorting. I'm assuming there's a difference between the two.", "I'd also like to add an interesting point here, because it's not that he just has an issue or perhaps difficulties with reading EEGs, but he also has difficulties with reading hadiths. The narration that he's quoting says the narrator said \"like a camel\". And the narrator here, what's that referring to? It's referring to a late narrator in the hadith, most likely [Hammam], which is quite late. You see, [Hammam] is narrating from [Ata] from safwan from [Ya'la], and [Hammam] is the only person here who may have narrated that, since other students of [Ata] did not narrate this from hamam. So this may be an explanation from someone from the early 2nd century. So even the mentioning of the camel, it's an explanation or a comment by a late narrator.",), "Farid Responds, “Neurologist debunks ex-Muslim! [Epilepsy Argument Deflated]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sejJCQYAvaI", ((2135, "35:35–35:59"), (2269, "37:49–38:58"),), id: 2320)
