#passage(("The allegation that the prophet peace be upon him had an [epigastric] rising sensation: epigastric is the upper abdomen, and basically it's a sensation that's rising upwards. It could be pain, it could be nausea; it depends on the patient. Now for those of you that are familiar with the [Sirah], surely nothing comes to mind. There's nothing that comes to mind about the prophet peace be upon him saying \"I felt something within me, a rising sensation\". There's nothing like that in the books of hadith or in the works of seerah, but that's because you're not as brilliant as Dr [Jalaluddeen], who has uncovered a great mystery that has been unsolved for centuries.",), "Farid Responds, “Dr. Jalaluddeen and the Chest-splitting Incident”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b16g5RNvltE", ((24, "0:24–1:06"),), id: 2321)
// U FR158:69-107 | Farid Responds | 4
#passage(("By the way, we actually do have an example of a companion that did feel the weight of the revelation. And [Zayd] didn't think of moving when he saw someone falling down, trembling, shaking, foaming the mouth? He didn't think, maybe I should get out of the way? He never thought of that. This is just too ridiculous.", "Let's just read the hadith. [Zayd] narrates that the prophet dictated to him: Not equal are those of the believers who sit at home and those who strive and fight in the cause of Allah. [Zayd] added: [Ibn Umm] Maktum came while the prophet was dictating it to me and said: Oh Allah's apostle, by Allah, if I had the power to fight in Allah's cause, I would. And he was a blind man. So Allah revealed to his apostle while his thigh was on my thigh, and his thigh became so heavy that I was afraid it might fracture my thigh.", "Look at the timing of the revelation itself. It occurs immediately after the prophet peace be upon him is asked a question. Then that state of the prophet peace be upon him passed, and Allah revealed: except those who are disabled. After receiving the revelation, he immediately provides an answer. Does this really look like a seizure to you? The idea of this being a seizure doesn't even come to mind. When you read this hadith, you understand that there's a supernatural event occurring, and their thighs are right next to each other because they're probably sitting in the cross-legged position, and because, of course, they're cross-legged and right next to each other, the thigh of the prophet peace be upon him is pushing down on [Zayd]. Revelation comes, it pushes down harder, and then when the revelation is done, it gets lifted away.", "The sheer desperation behind the arguments in itself should be proof for Islam. You want us to believe that the camel was trained to smell epilepsy and to sit down? There's more evidence of dogs being able to detect epilepsy. And once again, don't use a hadith if they can be used against you.",), "Farid Responds, “Dr. Jalaluddeen and the Seizure-Sniffing She-Camel”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8kzDeVSCj6U", ((323, "5:23–6:49"), (409, "6:49–7:31"), (451, "7:31–8:26"), (506, "8:26–8:58"),), id: 2322)
// U FR096:1-52 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("I found another book that I purchased years ago. This book refutes a modern argument, and of course this was not the intention of the author. This book is quite short, but it lists over 30 companions that sat in the back while he sat in the front and he drove. What's amazing here is that this refutes the argument that the prophet peace be upon him had epilepsy or that he had seizures. Well, how so? Because think about it: if you had a friend or a family member that had seizures, you wouldn't let them drive in the first place. In the States you need to be seizure-free for six months in order to be allowed to drive. And of course the claim is that the prophet peace upon him was receiving a seizure every single time he would receive revelation; that's the claim of those that are saying that he had epilepsy.", "And it seems like there's only one copy of this manuscript around, and I think it's called halet [Efendi]; that's the name of the library. It's in Turkey. So it's amazing to me how Allah defends the [deen] in such a way, by preserving a classical work, and then unintentionally refute something 900 years later.",), "Farid Responds, “This 900 year old book refutes modern argument!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=s0SxZDj5Bgs", ((11, "0:11–1:40"), (110, "1:50–2:31"),), id: 2323)
