#passage(("Now let's just check out the hadith for ourselves, and will inshallah come to a decision as to whether this is truly an [epigastric] rising sensation or not.", "Some months after our return, he and his brother were with our lambs behind the tents, when his brother came running and said to us: Two men clothed in white have seized that qurashi brother of mine and thrown him down and opened up his belly and are stirring it up. We ran towards him and found him standing up with a livid face. We took hold of him and asked him what was the matter. He said: Two men in white [raiment] came and threw me down and opened up my belly and searched [therein] for I know not what. So we took him back to our tent.", "Wait, so let me guess: did [Halima's] son also have epilepsy? Is this some sort of visual hallucination by [Halima]?", "Now for those who actually care about authenticity, here's an actual authentic hadith that you find in Sahih Muslim, in which Anas Malik is narrating that Gabriel came to the messenger of Allah sallallahu alaihi wasallam while he was playing with his playmates. He took hold of him and lay him prostate on the ground and tore open his breast and took out the heart from it, and then extracted a blood clot out of it and said: That was the part of Satan. And then he washed it with the water of [Zamzam] in a golden basin, and then it was joined together and restored to its place. The boys came running to his mother, i.e. his nurse, and said: Verily Muhammad has been murdered.", "It's not the prophet peace be upon him describing something he felt, but [it's] actual children seeing this happening. Unless they were all having visual hallucinations, we cannot use this as evidence for epigastric rising sensation.",), "Farid Responds, “Dr. Jalaluddeen and the Chest-splitting Incident”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b16g5RNvltE", ((135, "2:15–2:22"), (145, "2:25–2:59"), (179, "2:59–3:07"), (190, "3:10–3:47"), (230, "3:50–4:00"),), id: 2326)

=== 40.11 Miracle reports cited as seizure symptoms
// NODE P07.C04.S11
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR158:1-57 | Farid Responds | 5
#passage(("One of the strangest ways of making a case for Islam is by presenting the arguments against Islam, because the dumber the arguments are, the shallower they are, the more ridiculous they are, the stronger Islam seems to be. Because why would people be so desperate when it comes to their types of argumentation? Think about it: a false prophet is supposed to be really easy to expose, and yet we see these guys fumbling again and again, failing to argue for their theories.", "So the hadith that we're talking about is a narration that you can find in Ahmed, which says that when the revelation comes down upon the prophet peace be upon him while he's upon the camel, the camel itself would kneel down, would go down to the ground, due to the heavy weight of the revelation.", "First of all, these claims are unsubstantiated because of insufficient data. According to an article entitled \"Dog alerting and or responding to epileptic seizures: a scoping review\", we find that \"this review of scientific papers has revealed that this topic remains poorly investigated. Studies were mainly retrospective anecdotal reports with surveys that were prone to subjectivity, or case studies with a very small sample. Moreover, neither the training status of the dog nor its function were specified in all reviewed studies. It appears that appropriate empirical evidence that dogs can alert or respond to epileptic seizures is still missing. Further research is needed to better understand the characteristics of that determine a dog suitability, the aspects of reliability and specificity of an alert for different types of seizures, the mechanisms behind the alerts and the nature of the outcome of using dogs in context of epilepsy.\"", "Now, there are many other issues that come to mind. One of them is: let's assume that all this stuff is correct and that dogs can do all of this stuff. But those are dogs. What does that have to do with camels? Even if we assume that dogs could sniff out seizures, why assume that camels can do the same? More importantly, the dogs are trained to react. The same article states: \"Among 13 service dog training centers interviewed, training methods varied, but [reward-based] training techniques were applied if a dog began to alert, in order to reinforce this behavior. And training could take 6 months to 2 years to complete.\" So what does this mean? Did they train the camel of the prophet for 6 months before it was ready? What about this narration, when the prophet peace be upon him rode on the horse of Abu Talha? Oh, let me guess: I'm sure that the horse of Abu [Talha] was trained as well, just in case. I always thought that they didn't even know what epilepsy was back then.", "By the way, if you actually had epilepsy, it's quite unlikely that you would be able to drive a car; most countries won't even give you a license unless you've been [seizure-free] for like 6 months to two years. You know why: if you got a seizure while driving, you could potentially die. Of course, another thing that we know is that the prophet peace be upon him was receiving revelation all the time. He would be having seizures constantly. And what's really interesting is we don't find people preventing him from riding a camel. In fact, when he's sharing a camel, he's usually the person that's riding in front. Imagine being in the car with someone that constantly has seizures. Would you even let them drive?",), "Farid Responds, “Dr. Jalaluddeen and the Seizure-Sniffing She-Camel”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8kzDeVSCj6U", ((2, "0:02–0:34"), (34, "0:34–0:54"), (115, "1:55–2:53"), (173, "2:53–3:53"), (233, "3:53–4:37"),), id: 2327)
// U FR159:1-239 | Farid Responds | 6
#passage(("I'm going to be going through the arguments that have been brought forth by [Dr Jalaluddeen] in regards to the prophet peace be upon him having auditory hallucinations. The first thing that we're supposed to do is look into the authenticity of the reports, and remember, you don't need to be a scholar in order to catch the problems in the chain. Look at this, for example: apparently a scholar that we don't know said that this happened, and this isn't proof. It's not binding upon us, because we don't know who this person even is. So there's no reason to waste any more time with this one.", "Very good, Dr [Jalaluddeen], I'm impressed; I like that you're using an authentic hadith. So the prophet peace be upon him is stating that it's a specific rock; it's not something that's reoccurring. You'd expect that, with the constant seizures that are occurring to the prophet peace be upon him, he would be hearing rocks speaking to him all the time, trees speaking to him all the time. However, no, he's just saying there was a rock that used to greet me back then, thirteen, fifteen, twenty years ago. Notice that rasulullah is speaking to [Jabir bin Samurah]. So [Jabir bin Samurah] was quite young. This, as the narration implies, is occurring in Medina. So the prophet peace [upon him] is telling [Jabir] that around 15 or 20 years ago, maybe even more. With the continuous seizures that are supposed to be occurring (remember, you have 6 thousand verses in the Quran; that's a lot of seizures), you'd expect him to have a lot more auditory hallucinations. So that's the first point.", "You see, in the same chapter Dr [Jalaluddeen] is ignoring a report in which Ali says that he was hearing the trees and stones saying salam. So why didn't you read this report? Is it because you didn't want us to assume that Ali also had epilepsy? Maybe some other companions that were also there also had epilepsy, because there are many companions that narrate things like this about the supernatural. However, I like to be consistent, and I'm not going to be using the report by Ali, because that narration isn't authentic. But the same applies to the narration that Dr [Jalaluddeen] quoted, which is suffering from a disconnection.", "But yes, there is a major issue that doctor [Jalaluddeen] has been ignoring, which is that there are a lot of reports in which the prophet peace be upon him isn't the only person hearing things, but the companions that are narrating these reports are hearing them as well. [Jabir]: The roof of the mosque was built over trunks of date palms working as pillars. When the prophet peace be upon him delivered the sermon, he used to stand by one of those trunks till the pulpit was made for him, and he used it instead. Then we heard the trunk sending a sound of a pregnant she camel, till the prophet came to it and put his hand over it; then it became quiet, as if the prophet peace be upon him was consoling the tree trunk.", "Now as strange as that hadith is, it's narrated by multiple companions, including abdullah ansari, who I've just mentioned; [Ibn] omar, by the way, is narrating this in Iraq; and Ibn Abbas, who's narrating it in Makkah; and that's only the authentic chains. It's also been reported by [Buraydah], malik. Now keep in mind, these are people that are saying that they were actually there, that they witnessed this, that they actually heard these sounds. These are narrators that were narrating this specific event even though they were in different places, to different people. With that in mind, only an idiot would claim that this is evidence for some sort of auditory hallucination.", "To be fair, this is an old clip; it's about four years ago, and he stopped using this report because it's evidence for Islam, not evidence against Islam. But the question is: how does Dr [Jalaluddeen] feel about these reports? Because here's the thing: if the prophet peace be upon him hears something, then that's evidence for epilepsy, but if the sahaba hears something, oh no, that's supernatural, that's gotta be a fabrication, we can't trust that, that needs to be a lie. Similarly, if the prophet peace upon him sees something supernatural, then, oh, this is evidence for epilepsy, because that's a visual hallucination; but if the sahaba see something supernatural, oh no, this is clearly a fabrication, we can't touch this. And the thing is that any time that doctor [Jalaluddeen] brings some sort of naturalistic explanation by using one single hadith, then I will be providing much more that would suggest that no, this is a supernatural matter. So let's just be consistent and let's just say that all the companions had epilepsy. Please stop using evidence that I'll be flipping around and turning against you, because every single time you do that, you lose credibility.",), "Farid Responds, “Now they'll say the Sahaba had Epilepsy!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bUSIAAwMB8Y", ((1, "0:01–2:05"), (137, "2:17–3:24"), (250, "4:10–4:47"), (287, "4:47–5:38"), (340, "5:40–6:39"), (453, "7:33–8:51"),), id: 2328)
// U FR257:1110-1148 | Farid Responds | 1
